# Protocol changelog

Operational history of the Coverloop Multi-Model Production Protocol. The live doc (`CLAUDE.md`) carries only the current version; the story lives here.

## 2026-10-27 — protocol-selftest flags a full-suite `TEST_CMD` for pytest, go, cargo, pnpm/yarn and more (tooling only; no protocol change)

- The Stop hook runs `TEST_CMD` on every code-change stop. The hook sets no time limit of its own; only Claude Code's
  hook timeout stops it (runs were cut at 600 s on astro-lab), so a slow suite can cost up to that on every stop.
  `protocol-selftest` warned about that
  only for `npm test`, `vitest` and `jest`. A project whose `loop.conf` said `uv run pytest -q` (astro-lab, 2,263 tests)
  therefore passed the check as "verify it's cheap" and paid a full run per stop.
- The warning now also covers pytest, `go test`, `cargo test`, `npm/pnpm/yarn test`, mocha, phpunit, rspec,
  `manage.py test` and unittest. A runner that is already narrowed is accepted: `pytest -m/-k/--lf/--testmon`,
  `jest --onlyChanged/--changedSince`, `vitest --changed`. Warning only: nothing is blocked, and `loop.conf` is a
  convenience gate, not the Coverloop gate (`.coverloop/config.json` is unchanged and still decides the evidence).
- A chained command (`a && b`, `a; b`, `a | b`) is judged as a whole, flags match as whole tokens (`--lfnf` is not `--lf`),
  and `-kexpr` / pytest node ids count as explicit selections. Found by the Codex round on this change (3 × P2).
- Tests: `tests/test_hooks.sh` pins 8 flagged, 5 narrowed, 3 not-mis-accepted and 2 cheap commands (guard-break: removing
  the pytest pattern fails 4 of them).

## 2026-10-11 — the baseline walk has no cap (PROTOCOL v2.12.4)

Found in the field (astro-lab, 2026-10-11). To find the evidence baseline
for the floor, the gate validated at most the newest **100** evidence
commits, within the newest **2000** commits. Astro Lab's only approved L3
report (the T6 migration) sat behind more than 100 later reports, so the walk
never reached it and fell back to the repo's first commit. "Changes since the
baseline" then meant the whole history, migration included, and **every
`attest` and every `gate` run without `--base` floored at L3**. A docs-only
PR was refused. Each merged PR adds reports, so once an anchor slid out of the
window it never came back. A gate run with `--base origin/main` was not
affected. The cap failed closed, so this was a liveness bug, not a fail-open.
The same cap also broke a repo with no L3 at all: with more than 100 honest L1
reports, the oldest one in the window had to cover a 10+-file segment, which
is L2 by breadth, so the chain never validated.

- **The walk is bounded by history, not by a count.** Both the 100-report cap
  and the 2000-commit `rev-list` limit are gone.
- **It stops at an anchor**: the newest L3 report that passes its own
  evaluation (tests, Codex, guard-break, human approval). L3 covers every
  floor, and `evaluate()` does not depend on earlier reports, so such a report
  validates whatever precedes it. On any history git can read, the chain from
  the anchor onward is exactly what replaying every report from the root
  gives. A test checks this on random histories, including merged side
  branches with skewed commit dates, against a reference: the v2.12.3 walk
  with its caps removed.
- **Fail-closed is unchanged for everything the floor depends on** (one
  deliberate exception for history before the anchor is described next).
  Only an L3 report anchors. A lower tier's
  validity depends on the segment it governs. A report that would not count
  up close does not count from far away either. With no valid report
  anywhere, the floor still covers everything from the root, the root's own
  files included.
- **One deliberate difference from a full replay:** history *before* the
  anchor is not read, so a git object there that cannot be read no longer
  makes the floor unknowable. The full replay would refuse, and so did
  v2.12.3 when such an object fell within its 100-report window. It never
  read history beyond that window, so those reads were never a whole-history
  integrity check. What the floor depends on, `anchor..HEAD`, must still be
  readable, exactly as before. A test pins this
  (`test_history_before_the_anchor_is_not_read`).
- **Cost.** One `git diff` per report newer than the anchor, or per report in
  history when there is no anchor. That is the same per-report cost as
  before, but no longer capped at 100. Measured on a Mac: about 33–53 ms per
  report, so 1000 reports with no L3 anchor take about 50 s. A repo replays
  only the reports since its last approved L3, so this matters only in long
  runs without one. `git rev-list` still lists the whole history before the
  walk starts. A history too large to list inside git's 30 s timeout makes
  the floor unknowable and the gate refuses, which is fail-closed.

**Known limits, unchanged by this release:** the walk evaluates historical
reports with the default human gate, so an L3 report that passed under
`--human-gate-scope irreversible` without approval never anchors or advances
the baseline. The walk also does not apply `--require-transcript` /
`--require-executed` to historical reports. Both pre-date v2.12.4.

**Upgrading:** nothing to do. A repo whose floor was stuck at L3 for this
reason gates at its real tier again. Operators who raised the cap by hand in
a local copy as a workaround (astro-lab: the builder's `~/bin/coverloop`)
should replace that copy with this release. The root-owned merge-gate copy
(`/usr/local/lib/astro-merge/coverloop`) still has the cap and needs the same
upgrade. Verdicts change only where the old walk could not reach a valid
anchor. On readable history, the walk accepts only baselines that a full
replay from the root would accept.

Tests: seven new tests in `BaselineWalkHasNoCap`
(`test_an_L3_anchor_older_than_100_reports_still_bounds_the_floor`,
`test_an_unapproved_L3_anchor_out_of_reach_still_floors_at_L3`,
`test_a_valid_report_below_L3_is_not_an_anchor`,
`test_a_failing_L3_newer_than_the_anchor_is_replayed_not_skipped`,
`test_without_an_anchor_the_whole_chain_is_replayed`,
`test_the_anchor_shortcut_matches_the_full_replay`,
`test_history_before_the_anchor_is_not_read`). The first and fifth fail on
v2.12.3. No existing test changed.

## 2026-10-08 — a recorded exit code must be a real integer (PROTOCOL v2.12.3)

Found by Codex reviewing v2.12.2 (P2). Report-shape validation checked
`findings_open` and `guards_broken` but not `exit_code`. Every exit-code check
compares it to `0`, and in Python `False == 0` and `0.0 == 0`, so a captured
record with `exit_code: false` passed even `--require-executed`, and `null`
read as "no exit code". The withheld-placeholder check still stopped this from
laundering a real failed capture, so it was not a P1.

- **A present `exit_code` on a codex, glm or mutation record must be a
  non-boolean integer**, or the whole report is rejected as malformed (fail
  closed, like every other malformed field). `false`, `null`, `0.0` and `"0"`
  are rejected. Negative integers (a signal-killed run) are well-formed and
  still fail as a failed run. A record with no `exit_code` (self-attested,
  attached) and `exit_code: 0` gate exactly as before.

**Upgrading:** nothing to do for reports `coverloop` wrote — `capture_run`
always records an integer. Only a hand-edited report changes verdict.

## 2026-10-08 — a failed reviewer run is never a pass: saved, inherited or gated (PROTOCOL v2.12.2)

Found in the field (astro-lab, 2026-10-07): 12 intermediate reports read
`status: pass` next to `exit_code: 1`, which misled both the builder and a
human audit. At their own commit the gate rejected them. This release's own
Codex review (two rounds) then showed the same records could gate **green**
after an evidence-only commit — so this is a gate fix, not only a display fix.

- **`attest --<record> pass --<record>-run CMD` saves `status: "fail"` when
  CMD exits non-zero**, for every record (codex, glm, mutation). It used to
  keep the caller's `pass`. The withheld-transcript placeholder, the recorded
  exit code, the stderr warning and `attest` exiting 1 are unchanged; the
  warning and the `recorded [...]` line now say `fail`.
- **Inheritance saves a failed run as a fail.** When `attest` carries evidence
  across an evidence-only commit, any record with a non-zero `exit_code` is
  rewritten to `status: "fail"`, whatever its source. Before, a failed capture
  whose transcript did not verify was dropped to `self-attested` *keeping* its
  `pass` — and the default gate accepts a self-attested pass (only
  `--require-transcript` / `--require-executed` refused it). Any later
  `attest` after an evidence-only commit turned a known-failed review green,
  and every further `attest` carried it forward.
- **The gate rejects any codex or mutation record carrying a non-zero
  `exit_code`**, whatever its `source` or `status` (`reviewer_check`). This
  closes reports an earlier version already carried forward, without
  rewriting them. It only makes the gate stricter: a record with no
  `exit_code` (self-attested, attached) or `exit_code: 0` gates exactly as
  before. GLM stays advisory and never changes the verdict.

**Upgrading:** a codex or mutation record that an earlier version carried
across an evidence-only commit — self-attested, `pass`, non-zero `exit_code` —
gated green and now fails the gate. That is the fix: re-run that reviewer and
re-attest it (`attest --<record> pass|fail --<record>-run "<cmd>"` or
`--<record>-log <file>`). To find candidates:
`grep -lE '"exit_code": -?[1-9]' .coverloop/reports/*.json`. It over-matches:
it also lists failed captures at their own commit (already rejected) and GLM
records (advisory — they never change the verdict).

Tests: five new tests (`test_failed_reviewer_capture_records_status_fail`,
`test_captured_status_follows_exit_code_for_every_record`,
`test_legacy_failed_capture_is_not_laundered_by_inheritance`,
`test_already_inherited_failed_codex_run_is_rejected`,
`test_already_inherited_failed_mutation_run_is_rejected`); no existing test
changed.

## 2026-09-23 — a guard-break pass must say how many guards it broke; plan reviewer → Kimi K3 (PROTOCOL v2.12.1)

- **`attest --mutation pass` requires `--mutation-guards N` (N ≥ 1)**, recorded as
  `guards_broken`. The gate fails a pass without it — including every record
  written by v2.12.0 — and rejects `findings_open > guards_broken`. Found by the
  plan-review pilot (all four models): `pass, 0 surviving` alone was satisfiable
  by breaking nothing. `--mutation fail` needs no count. The count is an
  attested claim (same trust as `findings_open`), not verified against the
  transcript: it closes the silent empty pass, not a deliberately false one.
- **Reviewer helpers default to Kimi K3** (`glm-review` 2.1.0; `GLM_MODEL` still
  overrides; the `glm-*` names stay). The privacy rule now names the reviewer
  the helpers route to, gated on a passing `--zdr-selftest` — not GLM-5.2 by
  name. CONTRACT_VERSION v2.10. A known-answer pilot — three historical
  design packets with bugs confirmed later, same ZDR route and prompt for every
  model — ranked Kimi K3 first on confirmed bugs found and fastest (~1 min), with
  no false P0/P1; Gemini 3.1 Pro named one root cause the others missed but ran
  2–3.5 min, past the helper's 120 s limit; GLM 5.2 / 5.3 found fewer. Three
  cases, single runs: directional evidence, not proof.
- Tests changed: every test that attested `--mutation pass` now passes
  `--mutation-guards`; six new tests cover G1–G5.

**Upgrading:** L3 reports whose guard-break record came from v2.12.0 fail until
re-attested with `--mutation-guards`.

## 2026-09-23 — guard-break evidence replaces post-code GLM at L3 (PROTOCOL v2.12.0)

Measured across the fleet (2026-06 → 2026-09): GLM failed operationally on
roughly one run in five, its adjudicated P0/P1 findings were mostly false
positives from code outside the packet, and the long review tails were design
errors found late. What caught what no reviewer did was execution — building,
running, and breaking each safety guard on purpose. Details and the criteria
this release was held to: `docs/DESIGN-NOTE-v2.12.md`.

- **New evidence record `mutation` (guard-break).** `attest --mutation pass|fail
  [--mutation-findings N] [--mutation-log FILE | --mutation-run CMD]`. Same
  transcript rules as a reviewer record; `findings_open` = breaks no test caught.
- **L3 requires `mutation` (pass, 0 surviving) instead of `glm`.** Codex, tests
  and the human gate are unchanged; L0–L2 are unchanged.
- **GLM is advisory at L2/L3**: printed whenever recorded, never hidden, never
  changing the verdict. Its role moves to reviewing the plan before L3 code.
- **`<sha>.mutation.log` is an exact evidence-artifact shape**, with the same
  whitespace-smuggling defence as the reviewer logs.
- **`init` repairs an existing `.coverloop/.gitignore`** by appending only the
  negations it lacks. Before this, an old file was never touched, so the
  "re-run init" advice `attest` prints for swallowed evidence could not work.
- **Operating contract:** guard-break is mandatory at L3; execute before review;
  two-round rule (a P1 open after two Codex rounds → stop and redesign).

Tests changed deliberately (they asserted GLM was mandatory at L3):
`test_L3_requires_everything`, `test_new_commit_invalidates_old_evidence`,
`test_report_sha_mismatch_fails`, `test_attest_cannot_silently_downgrade_tier`
(record `--mutation` instead of `--glm`), `test_pinned_tier_is_a_floor_not_an_override`
and `test_tier_floor_folds_all_flags` (the missing L3 check is now `mutation`).
Ten new tests cover C1–C8 and the `init` repair. This release's own
guard-break: 9 deliberate breaks of the new code, 9 caught.

**Upgrading:** a repo that pins Coverloop by SHA is unaffected until it moves
the pin. On upgrade, an L3 report that carries GLM evidence but no `mutation`
record fails the gate — record guard-break evidence for in-flight L3 work
before switching.

## 2026-09-04 — the session contract stops being a process manual (PROTOCOL v2.11.0)

`classify`, `attest` and `gate` are **byte-identical** to the previous release:
`git diff <prev> -- bin/coverloop` is empty. This release changes what the
builder reads at session start, and two defects in the test harness itself.

- **The SessionStart hook prints three lines of invariants.** It fires at every
  session start and after every compaction, so its text is read on every single
  session — which is exactly why the 6 KB version was the problem. It mentioned
  "gate" nineteen times and told the builder to classify every task, pick a
  model, run two reviewers, re-attest, keep a ledger, run a self-test, and end
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# protocol-selftest — ONE command that verifies the whole wiring (machine + project).
# Mechanizes the §12 install checklist and catches version drift. Read-only; never fixes.
# Run anywhere for the machine checks; run from a project root to add the project checks.
# Exit 0 = no FAIL lines (WARNs allowed). Exit 1 = at least one FAIL.
set -u
FAILS=0
ok()   { printf 'PASS  %s\n' "$1"; }
warn() { printf 'WARN  %s\n' "$1"; }
fail() { printf 'FAIL  %s\n' "$1"; FAILS=$((FAILS+1)); }

# ---------- locate the protocol repo (for the reference version) ----------
SRC=""
for c in "$HOME/protocol-loop" "$HOME/coverloop" \
         "$(cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")/.." 2>/dev/null && pwd)"; do
  [ -f "$c/CLAUDE.md" ] && grep -q "PROTOCOL_VERSION" "$c/CLAUDE.md" 2>/dev/null && SRC="$c" && break
done
if [ -n "$SRC" ]; then
  REPO_V=$(grep -om1 'PROTOCOL_VERSION: v[0-9.]*' "$SRC/CLAUDE.md" | grep -o 'v[0-9.]*')
  CONTRACT_REF_V=$(grep -om1 'CONTRACT_VERSION: v[0-9.]*' "$SRC/CLAUDE.md" | grep -o 'v[0-9.]*' || true)
  ok "protocol source: $SRC (PROTOCOL_VERSION $REPO_V, CONTRACT_VERSION ${CONTRACT_REF_V:-n/a})"
else
  warn "protocol repo clone not found (~/protocol-loop) — version cross-checks limited"
  REPO_V=""; CONTRACT_REF_V=""
fi

# ---------- machine: helper CLIs ----------
if [ -x "$HOME/bin/glm-review" ]; then
  FV=$("$HOME/bin/glm-review" --version 2>/dev/null | grep -o '"filter_version": *"[^"]*"' | cut -d'"' -f4)
  [ -n "$FV" ] && ok "glm CLIs installed (filter $FV)" || warn "glm-review present but --version unreadable"
else
  fail "glm CLIs missing from ~/bin (run install.sh)"
fi
if [ -f "$HOME/.config/openrouter/m3_enabled" ]; then ok "M3: ENABLED for this user (data_collection:deny, L3-only)"; else ok "M3: parked for this user (fail-closed)"; fi
if [ -x "$HOME/bin/coverloop" ]; then ok "coverloop gate installed (~/bin/coverloop — v2.7 enforcement)"; else warn "coverloop gate missing from ~/bin (run install.sh) — the enforceable gate is unavailable"; fi

# ---------- machine: hooks installed + wired ----------
# Manual-install layout. A marketplace plugin install runs its hooks from
# ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks instead, which this script does not inspect — see
# the known limitation in CHANGELOG.md. Reading CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT here was
# tried and removed: Claude Code exports it to plugin subprocesses rather than
# to a shell, so it is unset in normal use, and when it IS set the reference
# clone and the active hook can come from two different installations.
HOOKS_DIR="$HOME/.claude/hooks"; MISSING=""
for h in session-contract.sh pre-risky-git.sh loop-stop-check.sh capture-failure.sh; do
  [ -f "$HOOKS_DIR/$h" ] || MISSING="$MISSING $h"
done
[ -z "$MISSING" ] && ok "all 4 hook scripts present" || fail "hook scripts missing:$MISSING"
if [ -f "$HOOKS_DIR/session-contract.sh" ]; then
  # The shortened hook deliberately prints no version banner — a version string
  # is exactly the kind of per-session noise the shrink removed. Compare the
  # installed FILE with the repo's instead:
  # that is what "this machine needs a pull" actually means.
  if [ -z "$SRC" ]; then
    # NOT ok: a clone this script cannot find (anywhere but ~/protocol-loop,
    # ~/coverloop, or the executable's own parent) means the comparison did not
    # happen. Reporting PASS there let a stale hook through silently.
    warn "no protocol repo clone found — cannot verify the installed session-contract.sh"
  elif [ ! -f "$SRC/hooks/session-contract.sh" ]; then
    # A clone WITHOUT the reference hook is a broken clone, not "nothing to
    # compare" — reporting PASS there hid the one case where the comparison
    # silently stopped happening.
    warn "repo clone at $SRC has no hooks/session-contract.sh — cannot verify the installed hook"
  elif cmp -s "$SRC/hooks/session-contract.sh" "$HOOKS_DIR/session-contract.sh"; then
    ok "session-contract.sh matches the repo ($HOOKS_DIR)"
  else
    warn "installed session-contract.sh ($HOOKS_DIR) differs from the repo's — machine needs: cd protocol repo && git pull && ./install.sh"
  fi
fi
WIRED=$(python3 - <<'PY' 2>/dev/null
import json,os
try: d=json.load(open(os.path.expanduser("~/.claude/settings.json")))
except Exception: print("UNREADABLE"); raise SystemExit
evs=d.get("hooks",{}); want=["Stop","PostToolUseFailure","SessionStart","PreToolUse"]
# Validate DEPTH, not just key presence (full audit 2026-07-11): an empty array,
# a hook with no command, or a PreToolUse block with no Bash matcher used to pass
# as "wired" — a false green in the tool whose whole job is catching wiring drift.
def cmds(entries):
    return [h.get("command","") for m in (entries or []) if isinstance(m,dict)
            for h in m.get("hooks",[]) if isinstance(h,dict) and h.get("command")]
missing=[e for e in want if not (isinstance(evs.get(e),list) and cmds(evs.get(e)))]
pre=evs.get("PreToolUse") or []
if not any(isinstance(m,dict) and "Bash" in (m.get("matcher") or "") and cmds([m]) for m in pre):
    missing.append("PreToolUse:Bash-matcher")
print("OK" if not missing else "MISSING:"+",".join(missing))
PY
)
case "$WIRED" in
  OK) ok "all 4 hook events wired in ~/.claude/settings.json" ;;
  MISSING:*) fail "hook events not wired: ${WIRED#MISSING:} (run install.sh)" ;;
  *) warn "could not read ~/.claude/settings.json" ;;
esac

# ---------- machine: gh + codex sandbox ----------
gh auth status >/dev/null 2>&1 && ok "gh authenticated" || warn "gh NOT authenticated (clone/PR from this box limited)"
if [ "$(uname -s)" = "Linux" ] && command -v unshare >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  unshare -Urn true 2>/dev/null && ok "codex sandbox prerequisite (userns) OK" || fail "unprivileged userns BLOCKED — see docs/CODEX_SANDBOX_LINUX.md (root fix); do NOT bypass"
fi

# ---------- project checks (only when run inside a project) ----------
PROJ="$(pwd)"
if [ -f "$PROJ/CLAUDE.md" ] && [ "$PROJ" != "$SRC" ]; then
  if grep -q "Operating Contract" "$PROJ/CLAUDE.md"; then
    PC_V=$(grep -om1 'OPERATING CONTRACT (v[0-9.]*)' "$PROJ/CLAUDE.md" | grep -o 'v[0-9.]*')
    if [ -n "$CONTRACT_REF_V" ] && [ "$PC_V" != "$CONTRACT_REF_V" ]; then
      warn "project contract $PC_V != current CONTRACT_VERSION $CONTRACT_REF_V — resync the inlined block"
    else ok "Operating Contract inlined in CLAUDE.md (${PC_V:-version unknown})"; fi
  else
    fail "CLAUDE.md has NO inlined Operating Contract — the protocol will not load (see docs/SESSION_BOOTSTRAP.md)"
  fi
  [ -f "$PROJ/docs/MEMORY.md" ] && ok "docs/MEMORY.md present" || fail "docs/MEMORY.md missing (portable memory)"
  # grep -c already prints 0 (and exits 1) on zero matches, so `|| echo 0` made
  # N a two-line "0\n0" and broke the numeric test with a stderr error. Default
  # the empty/file-missing case instead.
  N=$(grep -c '^- ' "$PROJ/docs/MEMORY.md" 2>/dev/null); N=${N:-0}
  [ "$N" -gt 30 ] 2>/dev/null && warn "MEMORY.md has $N entries — consolidate (cap ~30; merge/retire stale)" || true
  [ -f "$PROJ/docs/REVIEW_LEDGER.md" ] && ok "docs/REVIEW_LEDGER.md present" || warn "docs/REVIEW_LEDGER.md missing (false-positive ledger + review log)"
  [ -f "$PROJ/docs/RISK_MAP.md" ] && ok "docs/RISK_MAP.md present" || warn "docs/RISK_MAP.md missing (or kept in Obsidian)"
  if [ -f "$PROJ/.claude/loop.conf" ]; then
    TC=$(grep -om1 'TEST_CMD="[^"]*"' "$PROJ/.claude/loop.conf" | cut -d'"' -f2)
    case "$TC" in
      *"tsc --noEmit"*|*typecheck*|*lint*) ok "loop.conf gate is cheap: $TC" ;;
      # A bare full-suite runner is too slow for a hook that fires on every code-change stop (astro-lab 2026-10: pytest on
      # 2,263 tests). A runner that is already narrowed (-m/-k/--lf/--testmon) is let through to the generic "verify" line.
      *pytest*|*"go test"*|*"cargo test"*|*"npm test"*|*"npm run test"*|*"pnpm test"*|*"pnpm run test"*|*"yarn test"*|*"vitest"*|*"jest"*|*mocha*|*phpunit*|*rspec*|*"manage.py test"*|*unittest*)
        case "$TC" in
          # A compound command ("a && b", "a; b", "a | b") is judged as a whole: one narrowed part must not excuse another
          # unrestricted runner, so it never reaches the "narrowed" branch. Flags are matched as whole tokens (no prefixes);
          # "-kexpr" / "-mexpr" and pytest node ids ("file::test") are explicit selections too.
          *"&&"*|*";"*|*"|"*) warn "loop.conf chains several commands ($TC) — each one runs on every code-change stop; keep every part cheap (typecheck/lint/a narrowed run)" ;;
          *pytest*" -m"*|*pytest*" -k"*|*pytest*"::"*|*pytest*" --lf"|*pytest*" --lf "*|*pytest*" --testmon"|*pytest*" --testmon "*|*jest*" --onlyChanged"|*jest*" --onlyChanged "*|*jest*" --changedSince"*|*vitest*" --changed"|*vitest*" --changed "*) ok "loop.conf gate is a narrowed run: $TC (verify it stays fast)" ;;
          *) warn "loop.conf runs a FULL test suite on every code-change stop ($TC) — use a cheap check here (typecheck/lint/a fast marker subset) and keep the full suite for the gate (coverloop attest --tests)" ;;
        esac ;;
      "") warn "loop.conf present but TEST_CMD empty" ;;
      *) ok "loop.conf gate: $TC (verify it's cheap)" ;;
    esac
  else warn "no .claude/loop.conf — test-green Stop gate dormant (opt-in)"; fi
  if [ -f "$PROJ/.coverloop/config.json" ]; then
    ok "coverloop gate initialized (.coverloop/) — enforce with 'coverloop gate' / required CI check"
  else
    warn "coverloop gate not initialized here (run 'coverloop init') — optional, enables fail-closed evidence"
  fi
  D=$(git -C "$PROJ" rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD 2>/dev/null || echo "?")
  ok "project: $PROJ (branch $D)"
fi

# ---------- reflect staging size ----------
RS="$HOME/.claude/reflect-staging.md"
if [ -f "$RS" ]; then
  L=$(wc -l < "$RS")
  [ "$L" -gt 500 ] && warn "reflect-staging.md has $L lines — reflect-and-save then let rotation trim" || ok "reflect-staging.md size OK ($L lines)"
fi

echo "----"
if [ "$FAILS" -eq 0 ]; then echo "SELFTEST: GREEN (0 fails)"; exit 0; else echo "SELFTEST: $FAILS FAIL(s)"; exit 1; fi

exec
/bin/zsh -lc '/opt/homebrew/bin/git diff HEAD''^1 HEAD; /opt/homebrew/bin/git diff HEAD''^2 HEAD -- bin/coverloop CLAUDE.md docs/GATE.md; cat .claude/loop.conf; rg -n '"'test_gate|test_hooks|test_properties|test_report|unittest' .github tests/test_hooks.sh tests/test_gate.py tests/test_properties.py tests/test_report_v2.py; PATH=/opt/homebrew/bin:"'$PATH PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 bin/coverloop classify --base HEAD''^2' in /Users/[REDACTED:user]/code/coverloop-release
 succeeded in 180ms:
diff --git a/CHANGELOG.md b/CHANGELOG.md
index 2ee08c7..e52276b 100644
--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -2,6 +2,22 @@
 
 Operational history of the Coverloop Multi-Model Production Protocol. The live doc (`CLAUDE.md`) carries only the current version; the story lives here.
 
+## 2026-10-27 — protocol-selftest flags a full-suite `TEST_CMD` for pytest, go, cargo, pnpm/yarn and more (tooling only; no protocol change)
+
+- The Stop hook runs `TEST_CMD` on every code-change stop. The hook sets no time limit of its own; only Claude Code's
+  hook timeout stops it (runs were cut at 600 s on astro-lab), so a slow suite can cost up to that on every stop.
+  `protocol-selftest` warned about that
+  only for `npm test`, `vitest` and `jest`. A project whose `loop.conf` said `uv run pytest -q` (astro-lab, 2,263 tests)
+  therefore passed the check as "verify it's cheap" and paid a full run per stop.
+- The warning now also covers pytest, `go test`, `cargo test`, `npm/pnpm/yarn test`, mocha, phpunit, rspec,
+  `manage.py test` and unittest. A runner that is already narrowed is accepted: `pytest -m/-k/--lf/--testmon`,
+  `jest --onlyChanged/--changedSince`, `vitest --changed`. Warning only: nothing is blocked, and `loop.conf` is a
+  convenience gate, not the Coverloop gate (`.coverloop/config.json` is unchanged and still decides the evidence).
+- A chained command (`a && b`, `a; b`, `a | b`) is judged as a whole, flags match as whole tokens (`--lfnf` is not `--lf`),
+  and `-kexpr` / pytest node ids count as explicit selections. Found by the Codex round on this change (3 × P2).
+- Tests: `tests/test_hooks.sh` pins 8 flagged, 5 narrowed, 3 not-mis-accepted and 2 cheap commands (guard-break: removing
+  the pytest pattern fails 4 of them).
+
